<Page
  title="The Life in the UK test explained: format, pass mark, fee and booking"
  description="What the Life in the UK test is, who needs it, how many questions there are, the pass mark, the fee and how to book. Plain-English guide with links to the official GOV.UK pages."
  heading="The Life in the UK test explained"
  lead="What the test is, what it involves and how to prepare, in plain English."
  updated={LAST_UPDATED}
>
  <p class="note">
    Waymark is independent and cannot book tests. Rules on booking, fees and ID can change, so always check the
    official page at <a href="https://www.gov.uk/life-in-the-uk-test" rel="noopener">gov.uk/life-in-the-uk-test</a>
    before you book or take your test.
  </p>

  <h2>What is it?</h2>
  <p>
    The Life in the UK test checks your knowledge of British traditions, history, government and everyday life. Most
    people applying to settle in the UK (indefinite leave to remain) or to become a British citizen need to pass it.
  </p>

  <h2>The format</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Questions</th><td>24 multiple-choice questions</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Time</th><td>45 minutes</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Pass mark</th><td>75%, which is 18 correct answers out of 24</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Fee</th><td>£50 (check GOV.UK for the current fee)</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Where</th><td>At an approved test centre in the UK, booked online at least 3 days ahead</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    The official list of question types is not published. Test-takers commonly report four kinds: pick one answer,
    pick two answers, true or false, and choosing which of two statements is correct. Waymark's practice questions use
    all four.
  </p>

  <h2>What you need to study</h2>
  <p>
    The questions are based on the official handbook, <em>Life in the United Kingdom: A Guide for New Residents</em>
    (3rd edition). It has five chapters:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>The values and principles of the UK</li>
    <li>What is the UK?</li>
    <li>A long and illustrious history</li>
    <li>A modern, thriving society</li>
    <li>The UK government, the law and your role</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The history chapter is the longest and has the most dates and names to learn. The official mix of questions per
    chapter is not published.
  </p>

  <h2>How to prepare</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Get the handbook and read each chapter once.</li>
    <li>Practise little and often: 15–20 minutes a day works better than a long session the night before.</li>
    <li>Take timed mock exams in the last two weeks, aiming to score well above 18 consistently.</li>
    <li>Spend extra time on dates, monarchs and the facts people often mix up.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Read our guide: <a href="/guides/four-week-revision-plan">a 4-week revision plan</a>, or try the
    <a href="/practice-test">free practice test</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Booking and test day</h2>
  <p>
    Book only through the official GOV.UK service. On the day, bring the identification you used when you booked and
    arrive early. See <a href="/guides/what-to-bring-on-test-day">what to bring on test day</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
